re more open than others to negotiate I  think this as there always has been a  difference between the Member States and  the European Commission I think the  European Commission has a priority for  the political ideology of Europe about  ever closer Union and the Four Freedoms  member states who have governments who  need to be reelected have to worry about  real jobs real prosperity and real trip  let's talk about the back stop because I  mean that was Parliament's message  wasn't it that they'd accept the deal if  the backstop was effectively gotten rid  of but that's just not gonna happen is  it I mean I was speaking to the Irish  Europe Minister last week and she was  absolutely clear that this is not  something that Ireland can possibly  negotiate on well if they won't  negotiate on it then they are likely to  end up with no deal and the European  Union said that's the best route to get  a hard border so for the Irish I think  it's even more important than most that  they are willing to talk to us about  what the alternative ways are of  achieving no hard border because that's  the stated position of both the Irish  government the British government and  indeed the European Union one of the  things that may be a price for changes  to the backstop is the customs union  it's something that apparently  jean-claude Juncker has floated it's  certainly something that Labour are  pushing for if the customs union  something that you could stomach to get  a deal over the line no would you resign  well I think it would not fulfill the  promise we made to leave the European  Union we would not be taking back  control of our trade policy you cannot  have an independent trade policy if  you're in a customs union turkey finds  itself in that position at the moment it  can't even talk to the United Kingdom  but what might happen afterwards because  it's locked in a partial customs union  with the EU  so would you resign I thought the word  become well and I do think so I don't I  don't things about us I mean that makes  it about the politicians not about the  policy and I think we should concentrate  on the policy and I think that would be  a bad policy for the United Kingdom and  the other thing I'm very keen to Oishi  about is an extension to article 50 and  because this is something that has  increasingly been talked about because  frankly there's still so much stuff to  get done if there's gonna be any chance  of leaving the EU on the day that the  government wants to happen now  when we spoke in June you said this  about an extension of article 50 I  wouldn't find that politically  acceptable and I couldn't support it is  that your position still well that was  in the context of as not having reached  an agreement and simply extending the  time I don't think that solves anything  if we actually have an agreement and it  takes a little more time to get the  legislation through to make that as  smooth as possible I think that is a  very different argument it sounds a bit  like you're dancing on the head of a pin  that no I think there's a big difference  between if we have an agreement and we  need some time to get the legalities  done that's that's one thing I think to  extend simply because we hadn't reached  an agreement would not provide any  impetus for that agreement to be reached  and in any case there's no guarantee  that you would want to do that we're not  ready for no deal are we frankly well we  would be able to deal with that scenario  but it wouldn't be in our interest to go  there it seems to me we've got to guard  against two things one is irrational  pessimism that says you know everything  will be a catastrophe and irrational  optimism which says everything will be  absolutely ok the truth lies between the  two if we left the European Union with  no dealer would be disruption to our  trade the disruption to European trade  as well it wouldn't be in our best  interest to do that of course it's  survivable but we don