ocky period to say  the least I mean look at the way that  the u.s. is blocking or trying to block  charges to the WTO I mean is this really  the right time to be relying on this  organization yes and it's in fact time  to help strengthen the WTO and update  the WTO there are a whole range of  issues you mentioned just a couple of  them but there are others the fact that  we have not liberalized services the way  we've liberalized goods the fact that  even the differentiation between what is  a good and what's the services blurred  and yet the mechanics around governance  are not clear I said to my Chinese  counterpart some time ago I said if I  had asked you the question in 1995 if I  sell you a digital code over the  internet to make something on your 3d  printer have I sold you a good or a  service you even have understood that  question in 1995 and yet we're using the  same rules to govern the global economy  when that global economy itself is  fundamentally different so we do need to  update the WTO and as we take up our  independent seat after we leave the EU  that will be one of our great priorities  do you worry though the way that some  supporters of brexit talk about the WTO  like this is the fix-all solution to any  issues we might have if we  leave the EU without a deal well most of  our trade most of world trade operates  under WTO terms are the trade bills -  something like that is my point indeed  the if WTO was was so good people  wouldn't be looking to have trade  agreements or customs unions which are  ways in which you can further improve on  those WTO rules and it's always seemed  to me a bit strange that people would  say well we don't need to worry about  having a future trade deal with Europe  we can operate on WTO terms while at the  same time saying we should have a free  trade agreement with United States to  get away from WTO rules so we have to be  consistent do you think and that Therese  may was right or wrong to decide not to  call on 